<!doctype html>
<!--
  Template. Do not edit www/dist/index.html by hand.
  Double-brace tokens are substituted from repo-root plus-pricing.json
  by www/build.mjs. An unsubstituted token is a build failure.

  Claims discipline: every capability shown here is grounded in shipped code.
  Ask search is literal keyword/tag scoring, not semantic. Writes from chat are
  queued, not instant. Resurfacing happens when you open the app, never as a
  push. Do not soften any of those into something they are not.
-->
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Atoms · Notes you can ask</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Atoms turns the captures in your Obsidian daily notes into a connected library, then lets you ask it from Claude or ChatGPT. Your words stay verbatim."
    />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="dark light" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Atoms · Notes you can ask" />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="You already wrote it down. Atoms makes it findable again."
    />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://tryatoms.app" />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://tryatoms.app" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles.css" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <!-- 1. Hero: lead with the payoff, not the plumbing -->
      <header class="hero">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">Atoms · An Obsidian plugin</p>
          <h1>You already wrote it down.<br />Now you can ask it.</h1>
          <p class="lead">
            Atoms turns the captures sitting in your daily notes into a
            connected library, then lets you search it, quote it, and add to it
            from Claude or ChatGPT.
          </p>

          <div class="specimen">
            <div class="file file--chat">
              <span class="file-name">Claude</span>
              <pre class="file-body"><span class="who">You</span>
<span class="verbatim">what did I land on for trial length?</span>

<span class="who">Claude</span>
From your notes:

<span class="quoted">"people ask how long the trial is before
they ask what it costs"</span>
<span class="indent"><span class="sentinel">↳</span> <span class="wl">[[Trial length is the first pricing question]]</span></span>

A later atom revises it. You settled on {{trialDays}} days.
<span class="indent"><span class="sentinel">↳</span> <span class="wl">[[14 days is long enough to form the habit]]</span></span></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="file file--chat file--queued">
              <pre class="file-body"><span class="who">You</span>
<span class="verbatim">add: trial should start at first filing, not install</span>

<span class="who">Claude</span>
Queued for your vault. It lands next time Obsidian
is open.
<span class="indent"><span class="sentinel">↳</span> <span class="wl">[[Trial starts at first filing, not install]]</span> <span class="marker">pending</span></span></pre>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="actions">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="#install">Get Atoms</a>
            <a class="btn btn--secondary" href="#how">How It Works</a>
          </div>
          <p class="note">
            Free with your own Anthropic key. Plus is optional.
          </p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <!-- 2. The problem -->
      <section class="band">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="claim">
            A note you cannot find again is a note you did not take.
          </p>
          <p>
            Capture was never the hard part. You have years of it. The hard part
            is that none of it comes back to you at the moment it would have
            been useful. Atoms is built around that second half.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 3. How it works -->
      <section class="band" id="how">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
          <h2>Three steps, and you only do the first one.</h2>

          <ol class="steps">
            <li>
              <span class="sentinel" aria-hidden="true">↳</span>
              <div>
                <h3>You capture, however you already do</h3>
                <p>
                  A bullet in today's daily note, a shortcut on your phone,
                  anything that drops text into Obsidian. Atoms does not give
                  you a capture box and does not ask you to change how you
                  write.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="sentinel" aria-hidden="true">↳</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Atoms files past days into linked notes</h3>
                <p>
                  Run Process and Atoms reads captures from previous days,
                  giving each one a declarative title, tags, and links to
                  related notes. Today is skipped by default, so writing in the
                  moment stays quiet.
                </p>

                <div class="specimen specimen--inline">
                  <p class="stage">Before</p>
                  <div class="file">
                    <span class="file-name">Daily/2026-07-24.md</span>
                    <pre class="file-body"><span class="verbatim">- people ask how long the trial is before they ask what it costs</span>
<span class="verbatim">- pick up milk</span></pre>
                  </div>

                  <p class="stage">After Process</p>
                  <div class="file">
                    <span class="file-name">Daily/2026-07-24.md</span>
                    <pre class="file-body"><span class="verbatim">- people ask how long the trial is before they ask what it costs</span>
<span class="indent"><span class="sentinel">↳</span> <span class="wl">[[Trial length is the first pricing question]]</span> <span class="marker">&lt;!--linker--&gt;</span></span>
<span class="verbatim">- pick up milk</span>
<span class="indent"><span class="marker">&lt;!--linker:noise--&gt;</span></span></pre>
                  </div>

                  <div class="file">
                    <span class="file-name"
                      >Atoms/Trial length is the first pricing question.md</span
                    >
                    <pre class="file-body"><span class="marker">created: 2026-07-24
source: "[[2026-07-24]]"
tags: [pricing]</span>

<span class="verbatim">people ask how long the trial is before they ask what it costs</span>

The trial question lands before the price question (<span class="wl">[[Pricing]]</span>).</pre>
                  </div>

                  <p class="caption">
                    Your original line is never rewritten. Atoms appends a
                    marker under it and copies the text into the new note
                    unchanged, word for word.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="sentinel" aria-hidden="true">↳</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Then you can actually use it</h3>
                <p>
                  Ask it directly from Claude or ChatGPT, or let it bring things
                  back to you on its own. Both are below.
                </p>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 4. Recall, pull -->
      <section class="band" id="ask">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">Recall, when you go looking</p>
          <h2>Ask your notes from Claude or ChatGPT</h2>
          <p>
            Atoms runs a connector your assistant can use. Turn it on and your
            atoms become something you can interrogate in the chat window you
            already have open.
          </p>

          <ul class="facts">
            <li>
              <strong>It searches your atoms</strong> by title, tags, and text,
              and pulls the full note before quoting it. Answers cite the atom
              they came from, so you can open the original.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>It follows your links.</strong> Ask what connects to
              something and it walks the links and backlinks around it, which is
              the part a plain file search cannot do.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>It can add to your library.</strong> Tell it to capture a
              thought and it queues a new atom for your vault. It lands next
              time Obsidian is open, usually within a minute.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>It never overwrites you.</strong> Revising a note creates a
              new atom linked to the original as a revision. Your earlier
              thinking stays intact and you can see that you changed your mind.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Connector URL:</strong>
              <span class="mono">https://plus.tryatoms.app/mcp</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="fineprint">
            Search is keyword and tag matching over your atoms, not a semantic
            model, so it finds the words you actually wrote. Only your flat
            atoms folder is reachable. Daily notes and the rest of your vault
            are not.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 5. Recall, push -->
      <section class="band" id="resurface">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">Recall, when you are not looking</p>
          <h2>Sometimes it comes back on its own</h2>
          <p>
            Open Atoms and it may hand you one thing you had forgotten. Not a
            feed. One card.
          </p>

          <ul class="facts">
            <li>
              <!-- Placeholder name only. Never ship a real person from a vault. -->
              <strong>"Also about Sam."</strong> You file something new that
              links to a person, and an older note about the same person comes
              back the next time you open Atoms.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>"On this day."</strong> Something you wrote on this date in
              a previous year.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>"Mind change."</strong> Two notes where you came to
              disagree with yourself, shown side by side.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>One at a time.</strong> Tap to open the note, or press
              Another to see something else. The same memory will not come back
              for a week.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="fineprint fineprint--legible">
            This only happens while you have Atoms open. There are no
            notifications, no reminders, no review queue, and nothing is ever
            due. If you do not open Atoms, nothing happens.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 6. What stays true -->
      <section class="band">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">What stays true</p>

          <ul class="facts">
            <li>
              <strong>Your words are the record.</strong> Capture text is copied
              into the atom unchanged. Titles, tags, and links are the only
              things generated.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Links, not folders.</strong> Nothing is filed into a
              hierarchy. Atoms land flat and find each other through
              reason-bearing links.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nothing destroyed.</strong> Processing is idempotent and
              re-runnable. Your daily notes are only ever appended to.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Plain files you own.</strong> Markdown in your own vault.
              Uninstall the plugin and every note is still there and still
              readable.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Desktop, iOS, and Android.</strong> A normal Obsidian
              plugin on all three.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Not a task app.</strong> No due dates, no checklists, no
              backlog quietly filling up behind you.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 7. Plus -->
      <section class="band" id="plus">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">Optional</p>
          <h2>Atoms Plus</h2>
          <p>
            Filing and Ask both run on an AI model, which needs an API key. Plus
            is the version where you do not have to deal with that.
          </p>

          <ul class="facts">
            <li>
              <strong>No API key setup</strong> on phone or desktop. Filing runs
              through our key instead of yours.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Ask is included.</strong> The Claude and ChatGPT connector
              is part of Plus.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>{{includedFilingsPerPeriod}} AI filings each month.</strong>
              One filing is one capture classified, not one note. Unused filings
              do not roll over, and your allotment starts over on your billing
              date.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Need more before then?</strong> A one time top up adds
              {{topUpFilings}} filings for ${{topUpUsd}}. It does not change or
              renew your subscription.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="fineprint">
            AI usage has a real cost. Your subscription helps cover that and
            supports ongoing development. Your library stays free either way:
            the rest of Atoms does not require a subscription.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 8. Pricing -->
      <section class="band" id="pricing">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">Pricing</p>
          <h2>${{monthlyUsd}} a month, or use your own key for free.</h2>

          <div class="cards-2">
            <div class="card card--plus">
              <h3>Atoms Plus</h3>
              <p class="price">
                <span class="amount">${{monthlyUsd}}</span>
                <span class="period">per month</span>
              </p>
              <p class="alt">
                ${{yearlyUsd}} per year · {{yearlyDiscountNote}}
              </p>
              <p>
                {{includedFilingsPerPeriod}} AI filings each month, plus Ask. No
                API key to manage. {{trialDays}} day free trial.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>Your Own Key</h3>
              <p class="price">
                <span class="amount">Free</span>
                <span class="period">forever</span>
              </p>
              <p class="alt">You pay Anthropic directly for usage.</p>
              <p>
                Add your Anthropic API key in Settings and filing works. Ask is
                a hosted service, so it is Plus only, or you can self host it
                from the repo.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="fineprint fineprint--legible">
            {{trialFinePrint}} You cancel from Settings inside the plugin.
            Unused filings do not roll over. Top up is ${{topUpUsd}} for
            {{topUpFilings}} filings, one time, and does not renew. Prices in
            USD.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 9. Install -->
      <section class="band" id="install">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">Install</p>
          <h2>Get Atoms</h2>
          <p>
            Atoms is not in the community plugin directory yet, so installation
            goes through BRAT.
          </p>

          <ol class="install">
            <li>
              Install
              <a href="https://obsidian.md/plugins?id=obsidian42-brat">BRAT</a>
              from the Obsidian community plugins directory.
            </li>
            <li>
              In BRAT, add the beta plugin
              <span class="mono">taihartman/obsidian-atoms</span>.
            </li>
            <li>Enable <strong>Atoms</strong> in Community plugins.</li>
            <li>
              Open <strong>Settings → Atoms</strong>. Add your own Anthropic API
              key, or enter your email and choose
              <strong>Start Free Trial</strong> for Plus.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p class="fineprint">
            Prefer to read the code first? Everything is on
            <a href="https://github.com/taihartman/obsidian-atoms">GitHub</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- 10. Privacy -->
      <section class="band" id="privacy-summary">
        <div class="wrap">
          <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
          <h2>Where your notes actually go</h2>

          <ul class="facts">
            <li>
              <strong>Your vault stays yours.</strong> Atoms are plain markdown
              files on your own devices. No account is needed to use the plugin
              with your own key.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Processing sends capture text to Anthropic.</strong> That
              is how classification works. On Plus it goes under our account, on
              your own key it goes under yours.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>We do not train on your notes.</strong>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Ask is opt in.</strong> Nothing is mirrored to the cloud
              unless you turn Ask on, and only the flat
              <span class="mono">Atoms/</span> folder is included.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>One direction only.</strong> The mirror goes from your
              vault to the cloud. Nothing is ever written back over an existing
              note body.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="fineprint">
            Full detail in the <a href="/privacy">Privacy</a> page.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

